The attached PDF consists of two research articles focusing on "Nano-11," a dendrimer-like α-D-glucan nanoparticle.

### 1. Analysis of the attached PDF:

The research investigates Nano-11, a positively charged α-D-glucan nanoparticle derived from sweet corn, as a vaccine adjuvant. Both papers highlight its strong immunostimulatory properties, comparable to or better than aluminum hydroxide adjuvant, in inducing robust antibody responses against protein antigens.

In *vitro* studies show Nano-11 is efficiently phagocytized by dendritic cells (DCs), leading to their activation, marked by increased expression of costimulatory molecules (CD80, CD86) and secretion of pro-inflammatory cytokines like IL-1β and IL-12p40. Nano-11 also effectively adsorbs negatively charged antigens.

*In vivo*, Nano-11 formulations enhance the immune response. It remains at the injection site, clears gradually over approximately three weeks, and induces a transient, localized inflammatory response primarily involving monocytes and macrophages, with fewer neutrophils and less long-term tissue damage compared to aluminum hydroxide. Crucially, Nano-11 facilitates efficient antigen delivery to draining lymph nodes, specifically targeting highly immunopotentiating migratory dendritic cells (MigDCs). No systemic distribution to other organs was observed, indicating a favorable safety profile.

